    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras are leaning into speed and favorable matchups today, anchored by **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (68.37) and **Caleb Durbin** (66.18), who serve as our highest-scoring options. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits significantly from a leadoff assignment against Cade Cavalli, with dynamic platoon splits (+14%) and park factors (+4%) propelling him into elite territory. Meanwhile, **Caleb Durbin** draws a soft matchup against Max Scherzer; the veteran's elevated xERA (5.10) combined with **Caleb Durbin**\u2019s ability to exploit slow delivery patterns\u2014a critical factor for our SB-focused strategy\u2014makes him a high-floor play. **Christian Yelich** (65.44) also earns a strong recommendation, as his +13% platoon edge against Roki Sasaki positions him as a high-value asset despite the moderate threat posed by the opposing starter.\n\nOur most difficult decision centered on **Josh Smith** (32.43), who finished well shy of the starting threshold due to a unfavorable matchup against Payton Tolle. While **Josh Smith** possesses the utility profile we value, his projected #8 spot in the order and a -15% dynamic platoon hit against the left-handed Tolle effectively neutralized his offensive viability. Elsewhere, the lineup construction remains cautious regarding risk: **Trea Turner** (60.86) and **Kyle Schwarber** (57.64) are both facing the difficult Taj Bradley. While **Trea Turner**\u2019s elite speed and stolen base potential keep him in the green, the -5% wind-in penalty at the stadium forces us to temper expectations for power-reliant bats like **Kyle Schwarber**. \n\nManagers should note that our lineup relies heavily on pending official configurations for **Gary Sanchez** (48.02), **Kazuma Okamoto** (48.17), and **Chase Meidroth** (57.50). While **Chase Meidroth** maintains a solid score due to an enticing matchup against Andrew Abbott, his output is contingent on securing that #6 slot. With **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, and 